<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">L'Isle-sur-la-Sorgue</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">L'Isle-sur-la-Sorgue is a town in Provence, south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, built on islands in the Sorgue river below the spring of Fontaine-de-Vaucluse. Part of the papal Comtat Venaissin until 1791, it held one of the four Jewish communities of the Pope's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. Its waterwheels powered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and paper mills, and it is now known for its antiques trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lisle-sur-la-sorgue.jpg" alt="L'Isle-sur-la-Sorgue" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M.Minderhoud · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%27Isle_sur_la_Sorgue1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
